---
export const title = 'Accessibility and touch';
export const order = 200;
---

<ul>
  <li>
    A hover card is a supplement. The trigger keeps its own action (a link still goes to the
    profile), and everything in the card must also be reachable without it, for example on the
    page the link goes to.
  </li>
  <li>
    The trigger gets <code>aria-details</code> pointing at the content. Screen readers announce
    "has details" and offer a command to read it, without reading the whole card on every
    focus as <code>aria-describedby</code> would. The trigger gets no
    <code>aria-haspopup</code> or <code>aria-expanded</code>: those promise that activating it
    opens the card, and it does not.
  </li>
  <li>
    Keyboard focus on the trigger opens the card after the delay; Tab then moves into the card's
    links and buttons, and Escape closes it and returns focus to the trigger. Focus that comes
    from a click or tap does not open it.
  </li>
  <li>
    Touch screens have no hover. A tap activates the trigger and does not open the card. If touch
    users need the preview, open it from a control of your own with <code>show()</code>.
  </li>
  <li>The card fades in and out, without motion when reduced motion is requested.</li>
</ul>
